# Abstract

**Efficient Vision-Language Alignment in VLMs**

Contrastive vision–language models acquire their capability by jointly training
two large towers on web-scale paired data, at compute and data costs
incompatible with many research and deployment settings. This dissertation asks
whether independently pretrained vision and language encoders can instead be
aligned while frozen, using only a small trainable module under a budget of
fewer than five million trainable inference parameters and a measured latency
constraint. MobileCLIP2-S0 is the compact jointly pretrained reference and
distillation teacher; OpenCLIP ViT-B/32 is the field anchor and same-allocation
latency control. Both were reconstructed locally so that checkpoints,
transforms, splits and hardware are matched rather than imported.

Early results were unexpectedly weak, so the first contribution is diagnostic
rather than architectural. Decomposing the training recipe isolated the
cross-batch memory queue — included to supply additional negatives — as the
dominant cause: although the encoders were frozen, queued embeddings were stored
downstream of a rapidly changing trainable projector, so historical entries fell
out of correspondence with the current model. Removing the queue recovered
roughly 19 percentage points of retrieval R@1 and motivated a preregistered
factorial over queue age, size and modality (22 conditions, 66 runs, three
seeds). Harm began after a single optimizer step and no tested non-zero age was
safe. Decisively, conditions with near-identical measured projector drift
differed in damage by up to 4.74 percentage points, and the text tower's
degradation response was approximately 7.2 times steeper than the image tower's
over the shared drift range. Drift is associated with damage but does not
determine it. This is the principal scientific finding: a boundary condition on
the slow-drift justification for memory queues in frozen cross-modal alignment,
reported without a claim to the complete causal mechanism.

With the recipe repaired, the remaining work asked how much performance was
recoverable. Learned-query aggregation over frozen token sequences substantially
outperformed mean pooling, showing that access to patch tokens is insufficient
without learned selection, and yielded M_T1: the strongest strictly frozen model
at 54.487% validation mean bidirectional R@1 with 2.896M trainable inference
parameters. Web-scale data did not automatically help, the bounded CC3M arm
failing to converge within its compute ceiling. Internal token merging reduced
latency but cost 5.69–15.95 points and was rejected rather than promoted, and
guarded hard-negative mining was terminated before training when its
preregistered feasibility rule declared the candidate set infeasible.

Because fully fixed encoders cannot adapt their representations to one another,
FreezeShift applied a preregistered bounded low-rank relaxation to the final
encoder blocks. Adapting both towers raised validation R@1 to 63.416% with
4.862M trainable parameters, inside the latency budget under a corrected
same-allocation study. On the sealed one-shot Flickr30k test, strictly frozen
M_T1 reached 52.90 ± 0.87% and dual FreezeShift 62.20 ± 0.45% across three
seeds, giving a measured 9.30-point cost of strict freezing. FreezeShift
retained approximately 91.2% of OpenCLIP ViT-B/32 at 32.5% of its parameters,
but only 79.5% of MobileCLIP2-S0 at 65.7% of its size.

Two diagnostics bound what that retrieval result means. A frozen-gallery
ranking probe shows the residual gap is predominantly mis-ordering rather than
failure to retrieve: image-to-text, 29.8% of queries land in ranks two to ten
against the anchor's 18.2%, while only 9.0% fall outside the top ten against
1.7%. Zero-shot classification, however, did not transfer at all — on
Oxford-IIIT Pets the aligned models reach 8–10% against MobileCLIP2-S0's 89.2% —
so COCO-trained retrieval alignment does not reproduce broad CLIP-like semantic
generalisation. The conclusion is correspondingly bounded: this work does not
beat CLIP or reach compact-reference parity. It establishes how far efficient
alignment of independently pretrained encoders can be pushed, quantifies strict
freezing as a bottleneck, localises the remaining retrieval gap, and surfaces a
more general training failure — stale negatives can become harmful immediately,
and in modality-dependent ways that embedding drift alone does not explain.
